# NetBox Provider

The NetBox provider manages objects in [NetBox](https://netboxlabs.com/oss/netbox/) 4.7 through its REST API.
Every CRUD collection of the NetBox 4.7 API is available as a resource (`netbox_<object>`), a single-object data source
(`data.netbox_<object>`) and a list data source (`data.netbox_<objects>`); see the resource index in the sidebar.

## Installation and versioning

Declare `source = "elliot/netbox"` in `required_providers` (see the example below) and run `terraform init`; the
provider installs from the Terraform Registry without any CLI configuration. Hosts without registry access can use
the network mirror, install script or filesystem mirror described in
[INSTALL.md](https://github.com/elliot/terraform-provider-netbox/blob/main/docs/INSTALL.md). Releases follow
semantic versioning; breaking changes, deprecations and the supported NetBox and Terraform versions are covered by
the [versioning policy](https://github.com/elliot/terraform-provider-netbox/blob/main/docs/VERSIONING.md).

## Authentication

Use a NetBox v2 API token (`nbt_<key>.<secret>`, NetBox 4.5+). Create one under *Admin → API Tokens* or with
`POST /api/users/tokens/provision/` and `"version": 2`. The token is sent as `Authorization: Bearer nbt_<key>.<secret>`.

Legacy 40-character v1 tokens are also accepted and sent as `Authorization: Token <token>`, but every run then shows a
deprecation warning: NetBox deprecated v1 tokens in 4.6 and removes them in 5.0. The provider tells the two apart by the
`nbt_` prefix; set only the token itself, without a `Bearer ` or `Token ` scheme.

## Rate limiting, retries and delays

All requests of a provider instance share one client-side token bucket (`requests_per_second`, `request_burst`),
an optional fixed delay per request (`request_delay_ms`, plus `write_delay_ms` for writes), and an optional mutex that
serializes requests (`serialize_requests`) regardless of Terraform's `-parallelism`. HTTP 429 and 5xx responses are
retried with exponential backoff and jitter (`max_retries`, `retry_wait_min_ms`, `retry_wait_max_ms`), honouring the
`Retry-After` header. Every setting can also be provided through an environment variable (see below).

